[sliver] The measured adapter wrench flat meets the rounded boundary of its captured hex. Its width follows the six-plane hex at the installed clock over the whole wrench band.
alternatives: Broaching that face into a circle removes the mating flat. Adjusting the clock moves all six fitted planes relative to the adapter.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/rear-equipment-caps/asse-snap-cap.step
click: x=-12.629 y=-0.470 z=55.318

[thin] The sampled pair crosses two adjacent exterior faces at the 60° corner of the ASSE adapter housing. In this section the cap’s flat rim meets the solid sloping flank all along the adapter. The number describes distance through that corner near its edge.
alternatives: A wall-thickness reading across the housing uses the opposing wrench-pocket face. Extending or flattening the exterior angle would change the full fitted cap outline relative to its measured adapter housing.
